{% doc %}
  Emits padding as custom properties, for a section or for a block.

  The file was empty. Sixty-eight call sites rendered it and got nothing back,
  so every padding control in the theme editor moved a slider that changed no
  pixels. `base.css` was already reading these properties with `0px` fallbacks,
  which is why the omission produced no error and no visible breakage — only
  settings that quietly did nothing.

  ## Two property sets, not one

  A section pads block-start and block-end only, and takes an explicit mobile
  override. A block pads all four sides and scales the desktop value down on
  small screens by a percentage. They are different shapes, so they get
  different property names, matching what `base.css` reads:

      --section-padding-block-start / -end          (+ `-mobile` variants)
      --block-padding-block-start / -end / -inline-start / -inline-end
      --block-padding-scale-mobile

  ## Why inline properties and not classes

  Padding is a continuous range, so a class per value is not possible. Emitting
  the `style` attribute here rather than at each call site keeps the property
  names in one file: they have to match `base.css` exactly, and sixty-eight
  hand-written copies is sixty-eight chances to misspell one.

  ## Zero values are omitted

  A declaration is only written when the value is non-zero. The fallbacks in
  `base.css` already resolve to `0px`, so writing `--block-padding-block-end: 0px`
  adds bytes to every block on the page to say what the stylesheet already says.

  Left and right are spelled `inline-start` and `inline-end` so they follow the
  text direction and flip automatically in right-to-left languages.

  Parameters:
    section  (object, optional)  A section settings object
    block    (object, optional)  A block settings object
    settings (object, optional)  Alias for `block`, for older call sites

  @example
    <div {% render 'spacing', block: block.settings %}>

  @example
    <section {% render 'spacing', section: section.settings %}>
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = section
  assign b = block | default: settings

  assign out = ''

  if s != blank
    if s.padding_top != blank and s.padding_top != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-start:' | append: s.padding_top | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if s.padding_bottom != blank and s.padding_bottom != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-end:' | append: s.padding_bottom | append: 'px;'
    endif

    comment
      Mobile is only emitted when the merchant set it. Left at 0 the stylesheet
      falls back to the desktop value, which is the documented behaviour of the
      "Leave at 0 to use 60% of the desktop value" hint on those settings.
    endcomment
    if s.padding_top_mobile != blank and s.padding_top_mobile != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-start-mobile:' | append: s.padding_top_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if s.padding_bottom_mobile != blank and s.padding_bottom_mobile != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-end-mobile:' | append: s.padding_bottom_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
  endif

  if b != blank
    comment
      Both spellings are accepted. Most blocks use `padding_top`; a few newer
      ones use the CSS-shaped `padding-block-start`. Reading either means a
      block does not have to be rewritten to gain working padding.
    endcomment
    assign b_top = b.padding_top | default: b['padding-block-start']
    assign b_bottom = b.padding_bottom | default: b['padding-block-end']
    assign b_left = b.padding_left | default: b['padding-inline-start']
    assign b_right = b.padding_right | default: b['padding-inline-end']

    if b_top != blank and b_top != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-start:' | append: b_top | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if b_bottom != blank and b_bottom != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-end:' | append: b_bottom | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if b_left != blank and b_left != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-start:' | append: b_left | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if b_right != blank and b_right != 0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-end:' | append: b_right | append: 'px;'
    endif

    comment
      The setting is a percentage; the stylesheet multiplies by it, so it has to
      arrive as a unitless fraction.
    endcomment
    if b.padding_scale_mobile != blank and b.padding_scale_mobile != 0
      assign scale = b.padding_scale_mobile | divided_by: 100.0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-scale-mobile:' | append: scale | append: ';'
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if out != blank %}style="{{ out }}"{% endif -%}
